# Fleet manifest: the single source of truth for cell -> host -> phone assignment. Install as
# /etc/cowabista/cells.manifest on EVERY transport host (identical copy). The guard refuses a cell whose environment
# disagrees with this file, whose host differs, or whose host already hosts another cell.
# Columns: <cell name = systemd instance> <hostname as `hostname` prints it> <assignment>
# Assignment: `auto`; a static scope such as `1-4`; or, only while migrating between the two, `auto|1-4` (the cell may
# start automatic, or static with exactly that scope, so an unexpected restart mid-migration is not refused).
# Migration: docs/AUTO_PHONE_ASSIGNMENT.md section 7; rollback: section 8. Change a row only through the `auto|<scope>`
# step, never from `auto` straight to a bare scope or back: a cell whose env no longer matches its row is refused on
# its next restart (exit 78) and does not run until the files are fixed. Install every change as a candidate file
# checked with the guard's dry run (CAMPAIGN_CELL_GUARD_DRY_RUN=1), then by atomic rename; check sha256sum on all
# hosts. Never restore an older manifest wholesale.
#
# Automatic assignment (V2-372, recommended): every cell is "auto" and its env sets CAMPAIGN_TRANSPORT_ASSIGNMENT=auto
# with no CAMPAIGN_TRANSPORT_PHONE_IDS. The live cells divide every sendable phone between them, a newly connected
# phone is sent with no edit here, and a dead cell's phones move to the others within seconds.
cell-1   host-c   auto
cell-2   host-d   auto
#
# Static scopes (legacy, and the end state of a rollback):
#   cell-1   host-c   1-4
#   cell-2   host-d   5-9
# with the same CAMPAIGN_TRANSPORT_PHONE_IDS in that cell's env. Reach them from `auto` only through section 8 of
# docs/AUTO_PHONE_ASSIGNMENT.md (`auto|<scope>` rows, widened first to cover every Connected phone, then each cell's
# env, then the prefix removed). Static scopes must be disjoint, and a phone in no scope is never sent (the runtime
# logs "Sendable phone has no transport owner" after 20 s).
# A replacement for a dead cell keeps the SAME cell name; only the hostname column changes if it runs on a new machine.
